from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3414';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 3, Definition 1 and graph-embedding complexity; Section 6, distinction from abstract automorphism groups; Section 7, Problems 22–23.'
refs.append(dict(id='full',title='Compressing Permutation Groups into Grammars and Polytopes. A Graph Embedding Approach — full version',authors=refs[0]['authors'],year=2020,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.05583',locator='Version 1, 15 January 2020: Definition 1, Sections 6–7 and Problem 24.'))
save(i,dict(title='Explicit permutation groups requiring superpolynomial graph embeddings',formal='Does there exist an explicit family of permutation groups G_n≤S_n with graph-embedding complexity gec(G_n)=n^{ω(1)}? Here gec(G_n) is the minimum m≥n for which a connected simple undirected graph X on [m] has [n] invariant under every automorphism and {α restricted to [n]: α∈Aut(X)}=G_n. Explicitness is taken in the standard generator representation: one deterministic algorithm, given 1ⁿ, outputs a list of permutations generating G_n in time n^{O(1)}. The lower-bound requirement is ∀c>0 ∃n_c ∀n≥n_c: gec(G_n)>n^c. The graph may have arbitrary degree and treewidth. The requested family is not prescribed to be the alternating groups.',definitions='[n]={1,…,n}, and S_n is the group of all bijections of [n], with composition as the operation. A list of generators denotes the smallest subgroup containing them, allowing products and inverses. Each generator is output as its table of n images, using binary integers in [n]; the polynomial running time also bounds the total output length. This explicitness convention makes a uniform construction of the groups precise, rather than asking to list all their elements. A graph X=([m],E) has E⊆{{u,v}:u,v∈[m],u≠v}, with no loops, multiple edges, directions or colors. It is connected when every pair of vertices is joined by a path. Aut(X) consists of all permutations of [m] preserving adjacency in both directions; numeric vertex labels are identifiers, not colors fixed by automorphisms. Invariance of [n] means α([n])=[n] for every α∈Aut(X). The restriction map from Aut(X) to S_n must have image exactly G_n. It may have a nontrivial kernel: automorphisms fixing all first n vertices may still permute the remaining vertices. It is not enough for Aut(X) merely to contain a subgroup isomorphic to G_n, and the whole Aut(X) need not be isomorphic to G_n. Define gec(G)=+∞ if no such graph exists, although the source recalls a finite O(n+|G|) realization for every G≤S_n. The asymptotic parameter is the permutation degree n, not the group order |G_n|, which can be as large as n!. The convention n^{ω(1)} means eventual growth beyond every fixed power of n, as quantified in the statement.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Provide a uniformly polynomial-time generator construction and prove the superpolynomial vertex lower bound for every connected simple graph realizing each G_n as the full induced action on its invariant first n vertices, or refute that existence assertion under the stated explicitness convention. A lower bound only for bounded-degree or restricted-treewidth graphs, for the abstract automorphism group, or for the stronger requirement that the restriction map be injective does not suffice. A family with only a fixed polynomial lower bound does not meet the superpolynomial requirement.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The action on distinguished vertices is what must be represented',text='A graph represents a permutation group here through all its automorphisms acting on an invariant subset. Extra vertices can carry symmetries invisible on that subset. The resulting quotient action is the object measured by graph-embedding complexity.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A known abstract-group theorem does not resolve this version',text='The source contrasts this question with Liebeck’s exponential lower bound for graphs whose entire automorphism group is abstractly isomorphic to the alternating group. Since the present representation allows a nontrivial kernel on the additional vertices, that abstract-group statement does not transfer directly.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The unrestricted host graph matters',text='The paper proves lower bounds when host graphs have specified restrictions on degree and treewidth. Its concluding question removes those restrictions. The symmetric group itself has a size-n representation by the complete graph, illustrating that a large group order alone does not force a large graph.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Editorial conventions and a source typo',text='The published Problem 22 says superpolynomial but prints n^{Ω(1)}; visual inspection confirms that this is in the PDF, not merely extraction damage. The surrounding discussion and alternating-group question make the superpolynomial intent clear, so this card uses n^{ω(1)}. The source does not formalize explicitness; polynomial-time output of generators is stated here as the standard computational convention.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Connections to succinct representations',text='The paper connects graph representations of permutation groups with context-free grammars and extension complexity of associated polytopes. A general lower bound would distinguish small descriptions of a permutation group from small graphs that realize exactly its prescribed action.',citation='primary')],why='Would show that some succinctly described permutation actions intrinsically require large graphs, clarifying the expressive limits of graph automorphism representations beyond abstract group realization.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='The paper establishes restricted-host lower bounds and leaves explicit superpolynomial lower bounds for unrestricted connected graphs open, alongside a separate alternating-group instance.',citation='primary')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the full embedding definition, the kernel-versus-abstract-group distinction, conference concluding questions and full version. Inspected the printed asymptotic typo on PDF page 13. Exact-title, embedding-complexity and alternating-group searches through 12 September 2026 found no later resolution. Explicitness is a disclosed generator-model convention, not a quoted source definition.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A representation-size problem connecting permutation actions, automorphism groups and succinct computational descriptions. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the unrestricted group-family question, connected simple hosts, invariant subset and exact induced action with a possible kernel. Corrected the source’s asymptotic typo according to its repeated verbal target and made the generator explicitness convention visible.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The target is an explicit family of groups acting on n labeled points.','A graph represents the group when all its automorphisms preserve those points and induce exactly the given permutations on them.','Additional vertices and automorphisms acting trivially on the original points are allowed.','The question asks whether every such connected graph must have more than polynomially many vertices for a uniformly constructible family.','Known abstract-group and restricted-host lower bounds address different requirements and do not settle this target.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full'])),notes=['Recovered the full explicit group-family target and permutation-degree parameter.','Defined connected simple hosts, setwise invariance, exact restriction image and possible kernel.','Made polynomial-time generator explicitness explicit as an editorial convention.','Corrected the printed n^{Ω(1)} typo using the repeated superpolynomial intent, after visual verification.','Separated unrestricted embedding from abstract automorphism realization and restricted host classes.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
